<title>CHILE: Codelco copper output seen rising as of 1998.</title>
<headline>Codelco copper output seen rising as of 1998.</headline>
<dateline>SANTIAGO 1997-01-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>State company Codelco will continue to be Chile's largest copper producer into the next century, although private mining production will rise sharply, figures released by the Mining Ministry showed.</p>
<p>The forecasts show Codelco's output this year virtually unchanged from last at 1.19 million tonnes, then jumping to 1.41 million in 1998 as the Radomiro Tomic mine comes on stream and ramping up to 1.53 million by the year 2001.</p>
<p>Codelco's share of total Chilean mining output will fall to about 34 percent in 2000 from 40 percent currently.</p>
<p>State copper refining company Empresa Nacional de Minera, or Enami, will see production hold steady at this year's rate of 184,000 tonnes of copper at least through the year 2001, the figures showed.</p>
<p>Here are expected production figures for major mining companies in Chile, plus forecasts for the country's entire copper sector, as provided by the Mining Ministry.</p>
<p>COMPANY		   1996   1997   1998   1999   2000   2001</p>
<p>Codelco		  1,194  1,194  1,410  1,498  1,496  1,536</p>
<p>Escondida		  832    920    890    965  1,055  1,065</p>
<p> Collahuasi		  0	0    150    300    380    380</p>
<p> El Abra		    50    223    225    225    225    225</p>
<p> Candelaria		135    130    172    172    172    172</p>
<p> Disputada		 199    197    189    193    196    204</p>
<p> Santa Barbara	    81     80     80     80     80     77</p>
<p> Zaldivar		   77    110    125    125    125    110</p>
<p> Los Pelambres	    23     24     24    230    230    230</p>
<p> Quebrada Blanca	  65     75     75     75     75     75</p>
<p> Cerro Colorado	   58     60     60     60     60     60</p>
<p>TOTAL*		    3.07   3.39   3.84   4.46   4.70   4.74</p>
<p>*includes smaller mines not listed here.</p>
<p>	  --Roger Atwood, Santiago newsroom +56-2-699-5595 x211</p>
